#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Control service for the USB-AoIP web page.

Serves the page's static files and a JSON API for the stack this Pi runs
(the Dante endpoint, or the older AES67 bridge): health, meters, logs, a
preset store, and requests to change state.

It changes no device state itself. Every change (a profile or preset switch,
a preset install or removal, a live coefficient update, a commit, a
power-off) is a request file it creates in the applier's inbox
(USB16X16_APPLY_INBOX): uploaded artifacts first, then <id>.request. A root
path unit runs usb16x16-apply on the inbox, which checks each request again
against the live configuration, acts, verifies, rolls back on failure and
writes current.json in its state directory (USB16X16_APPLY_STATE), which this
process only reads. The inbox cannot be listed by this process: a request is
known to be pending by stat-ing the name it wrote.

Design constraints this file honours:

  * One collector thread owns every Pi read. HTTP handlers copy a cached
    snapshot and never shell out (the log route and the meter and apply-status
    reads excepted, each one bounded), so a slow or duplicated browser cannot
    multiply work against the audio stack.
  * Three refresh tiers: cheap file reads at 1 Hz, systemctl and sysfs every
    5 s, the AES67 daemon API or the Dante owner's journal every 10 s.
  * /api/sink/status/N is never read. Its arrival-lead minimum resets on read
    and the latency harness owns that counter.
  * No ALSA device is opened. Meters come from the snapshot file the bridge or
    the Dante owner writes.
  * Runs unprivileged. Nothing here needs root and nothing here is given it;
    the request is a file, and the privilege lives in usb16x16-apply.
  * HTTP Basic auth guards every route. Verifying a password costs a real KDF,
    so a successful credential is cached briefly and repeated failures are rate
    limited per client: the 2 s health poll must not turn into a PBKDF2 load on
    the machine running the audio.
  * State-changing methods (POST, PUT, DELETE) also need the X-USB-AoIP: 1
    header, a JSON content type when they carry a body, and an Origin that
    matches Host when a browser sends one, so another site cannot make a
    signed-in browser send them. Every response carries security headers.
  * It binds the management interface's address (or --bind), never a
    fallback, and serves HTTPS when given --tls-cert and --tls-key.
"""

